  "nameStory": "The Milwaukee Mining Bee gets its name from Milwaukee, Wisconsin, where this species was first scientifically described and collected by early entomologists. Like all mining bees, it earns the \"mining\" part of its name from its nesting behavior - females dig tunnels into the ground like tiny miners excavating shafts for their nurseries.",
  "description": "The Milwaukee Mining Bee is a solitary ground-nesting bee that's part of the large mining bee family. These are medium-sized bees, typically dark-colored with varying amounts of light-colored hair or bands across their bodies. Unlike honeybees that live in hives, each female Milwaukee Mining Bee works alone, digging her own tunnel nest in the soil where she'll raise her young. They're important native pollinators that visit a variety of flowering plants throughout their active season.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ground nesting behavior",
      "description": "Look for small conical mounds of excavated soil with a central hole about pencil-width - these are the entrance tunnels females dig for their nests",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Body size and build",
      "description": "Medium-sized bee with a robust, fuzzy body that's noticeably larger than a sweat bee but smaller than a bumble bee",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Coloration",
      "description": "Generally dark brown to black body with areas of lighter-colored hair that may appear grayish or pale brown, especially on the thorax",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Flies with a steady, purposeful buzz - not as erratic as hover flies but more direct than bumble bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flower visiting behavior",
      "description": "Often seen methodically working flowers, spending more time per bloom than honey bees, with pollen visible as dusty patches on their fuzzy bodies",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Milwaukee Mining Bees prefer areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soils where females can easily excavate their nesting tunnels. Look for them in sunny open areas like meadows, field edges, gardens, and parks where flowering plants are abundant. They're often found in areas with sparse vegetation where the ground is partially exposed, making digging easier.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge from underground and begin appearing at flowers. Look for fresh excavation mounds as females start digging new nest tunnels in suitable soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when you'll see the most adults visiting flowers and females actively provisioning their nests with pollen and nectar for their developing larvae.",
    "fall": "Activity winds down as adults complete their life cycle. New generation develops underground and will overwinter as mature larvae or pupae.",
    "winter": "No adult activity - the next generation waits underground in their nest cells, developing slowly until warm weather returns."
  },
  "funFact": "A single female Milwaukee Mining Bee can move up to 40 times her own body weight in soil while excavating her nest tunnel, creating an underground nursery that can extend 8-12 inches deep with multiple side chambers for her eggs.",
  "confusionNotes": "Milwaukee Mining Bees are easily confused with other Andrena species, which can only be reliably distinguished by microscopic features. However, you can separate mining bees from similar-sized bees by their ground-nesting behavior and the distinctive soil mounds they create - sweat bees and leafcutter bees don't make these characteristic excavation piles.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The name \"mining bee\" sometimes confuses people who think these bees might be harmful to gardens or lawns, but they're actually beneficial pollinators that cause no structural damage. Their small soil mounds disappear quickly and their tunnels actually help aerate compacted soil.",
  "compareHints": "Among the many Andrena species in New England, Milwaukee Mining Bee is distinguished mainly through technical characteristics visible only under magnification. In the field, focus on the combination of habitat preferences, timing of activity, and preferred flowers rather than trying to distinguish between closely related mining bee species. Most Andrena species have similar life cycles and nesting behaviors, making behavioral observation more reliable than attempting visual species identification.",
